    "knowledge_point": "Evaluating an Improper Integral of the Second Kind",
    "problem": "Evaluate the integral  ${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
    "worked_solution": "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{u}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u||}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2}{a}| \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent.",
